// SHARPENED DIAGNOSIS (v-inference, 2026-07-17) of what was filed as the "instantiation-PRESSURE
// CEILING" (mlrepro-pressure-ceiling-flatten-plus-gmap-tuple-tips-monomorphizer.cdz). It is NOT a
// superlinear monomorphization-cost/COUNT ceiling — it is the RECURSIVE-GENERIC MONOMORPHIZATION TIE
// (the flatten-fix family, 8dfa2074e) surfacing on `gmap`, with a check≡compile DISCREPANCY.
//
// MINIMAL TRIGGER (2 instantiations, NO flatten — flatten was a RED HERRING):
//   `gmap` (a recursive transformer threading a closure, scheme `(-> (GIter a) (-> (-> a b) (GIter b)))`)
//   at TWO instantiations with DISTINCT DOMAIN types where one RESULT is an AGGREGATE:
//     - int→tuple  `gmap(from-list([1,2]), fn(x) => (x,x))`
//     - string→string `gmap(from-list(["a","b"]), fn(s) => String.concat(s,s))`
//   => DECLINES CDZ0201 (recursive-generic tie). But:
//     - int→tuple + int→int (SAME domain int)        => PASSES.
//     - two DIFFERENT tuple shapes (int→(x,x), int→(x,x,x), same domain) => PASSES.
//     - 3 SCALAR gmaps at int/string/bool (distinct domains, scalar results) => PASSES.
//   So the load-bearing combination is: ≥2 gmap instantiations at DISTINCT DOMAIN element types AND at
//   least one AGGREGATE result. NOT raw count, NOT flatten, NOT compound-depth.
//
// KEY EVIDENCE:
//   (1) check≡compile DISCREPANCY: `cdz check` PASSES (0 errors); `cdz test`/compile DECLINES CDZ0201.
//       So inference (check) accepts it; the tie only bites at the monomorphize/emit stage.
//   (2) ANNOTATING the closure domains FIXES it: `fn(x: Int64) => (x,x)` + `fn(s: String) => …` => PASSES.
//       So the root is `gmap`'s closure-DOMAIN var `a` not being tied per-instantiation when two
//       distinct-domain instantiations coexist — the annotation supplies the missing per-instantiation tie.
//
// ROOT (v-inference domain): same family as the landed flatten tie (8dfa2074e, collect_param_constraints)
// but a DISTINCT shape — `gmap` threads the closure `f`'s domain to the element `h` of `it` (`(f h)` unifies
// f's domain with it's element), but across TWO monomorphizations at distinct domains + an aggregate result
// the domain var `b`/`a` link is not preserved per-copy. FIX is in the recursive-generic monomorphization
// seam (type_specialize / the tie solver) — HIGH-RISK hot path; design-adjacent to the greenlit
// forall-binder work (a type-var value flowing per-instantiation). Deferred pending careful design; NOT a
// pressure/cost problem, so the earlier "weight composite elements in a pressure metric" hypothesis is WRONG.
//
// ============================================================================================
// ROOT-CAUSE REFINED (v-inference, 2026-07-17 _w43) — the exact mechanism, via `cdz type gmap`:
//   - SINGLE-use gmap: scheme MONOMORPHIZES to (-> (GIter Int64) (-> (-> Int64 (Tuple Any Any)) ...)).
//     Note the closure RESULT is (Tuple ANY ANY) — the tuple elements are NOT tied to the closure param
//     x:Int64. A single monomorphization TOLERATES this Any (it grounds at emit) → PASSES.
//   - TWO-distinct-domain gmap: scheme stays GENERIC (-> (GIter a) (-> (-> a b) (GIter b))) (can't
//     monomorphize to one type), so `type_specialize` must bind a/b PER-CALL from the args. For the tuple
//     call the arg closure|s result is (Tuple Any Any), so b = (Tuple Any Any) — an Any-bearing type —
//     and type_specialize REQUIRES all arg/param types DETERMINED, so it REJECTS the Any → CDZ0201.
//   - ANNOTATING the closure param (fn(x: Int64) => (x,x)) keeps gmap generic BUT passes: type_specialize
//     binds a/b from the ANNOTATION, not the untied closure body.
// So the TRUE root is: an UNANNOTATED closure|s AGGREGATE-result element types infer `Any` (not tied to
// the closure param); this survives to emit for a MONOMORPHIC HOF use but is rejected by type_specialize
// when the HOF stays POLYMORPHIC (>=2 distinct-domain uses). FIX (deep, deferred): either (a) tie an
// unannotated closure|s aggregate-result element types to its param during inference, or (b) let
// type_specialize tolerate an Any that will ground at emit (the monomorphic path already does). Both are
// hot-path changes, design-adjacent to forall-binders. NOT a pressure/count ceiling; NOT flatten.
// ============================================================================================
// The 2-test program below reproduces the CDZ0201 via `cdz test` while `cdz check` passes clean:
type GIter = | Nil | Cons(a, GIter(a))
def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))
def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)
def gmap(it, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(f(h), gmap(rest, f))
@test
def t() = if count(gmap(from-list([1,2]), fn(x) => (x, x))) == 2 then unit else trap("t")
@test
def b() = if count(gmap(from-list(["a","b"]), fn(s) => String.concat(s,s))) == 2 then unit else trap("b")

// ===== RESOLVED on WASM (corpus-bugfix, 2026-07-20, trunk 213415220) =====
// The recursive-generic monomorphization tie — gmap at >=2 DISTINCT DOMAIN element types with >=1 AGGREGATE
// result — now COMPILES + RUNS. Verified:
//   gmap(from-list([1,2]), fn(x)=>(x,x)) + gmap(from-list(["a","b"]), fn(s)=>String.concat(s,s)) -> 4
//   + a 3rd distinct domain (bool, aggregate) -> 6.
// The type_specialize / tie-solver fix (the _w44 / flatten-tie family, 8dfa2074e neighborhood) landed —
// the closure-DOMAIN var is now tied per-instantiation across coexisting distinct-domain monomorphizations.
// This was the HIGH-RISK deferred item; the base direct-aggregate case is fixed. The remaining residual is
// the EXOTIC nested-generic-call-in-compound-closure-result (fn(x)=>(from-list([x]),x)), still parked at
// v-inference (queue/...closure-result-compound-containing-a-generic-call...PARKED-v-inference). NOTE:
// run-rust still gracefully declines (separate stored-fn-typed-closure class, parked v-inference) — not this
// tie; wasm is green. Marked RESOLVED.
